steps.message:
There is no built-in abstraction for `Environment`, so a small hand-written wrapper is the right call. I wrapped only the two members your code actually uses — `GetEnvironmentVariable` and `MachineName` — not the whole `Environment` API.

### Interface

```csharp
public interface IEnvironment
{
    string? GetEnvironmentVariable(string variable);
    string MachineName { get; }
}
```

### Default implementation (delegates to the real static)

```csharp
public sealed class SystemEnvironment : IEnvironment
{
    public string? GetEnvironmentVariable(string variable)
        => Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(variable);

    public string MachineName => Environment.MachineName;
}
```

I made it `sealed` because it is a leaf adapter with no intended subclassing.

### DI registration

```csharp
services.AddSingleton<IEnvironment, SystemEnvironment>();
```

`AddSingleton` is correct here because the wrapper holds **no mutable state** — it just forwards to static members, so one shared instance is safe. If you later add a stateful wrapper (for example one that caches per-request values), switch it to `AddScoped`; for a wrapper that must not be shared at all, use `AddTransient`.

### In tests

Inject a hand-rolled fake or a mock of `IEnvironment` that returns fixed values for `GetEnvironmentVariable` and `MachineName`, so the code under test sees a deterministic environment.
